You ruled out a DI container and cannot change the released static signatures, so the answer is an **ambient clock** stored in an `AsyncLocal<T>` — no constructor, no registration, and the public API stays exactly as shipped. `AsyncLocal` (unlike `[ThreadStatic]`) flows across `await` points, so parallel tests each see their own substitution.

### Ambient seam inside RetentionPolicy

```csharp
public static class RetentionPolicy
{
    private static readonly AsyncLocal<TimeProvider?> _clockOverride = new();

    // Real clock by default; a test overrides it flow-locally.
    private static TimeProvider Clock => _clockOverride.Value ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public static bool IsExpired(DateTimeOffset createdAt, TimeSpan retention)
        => Clock.GetUtcNow() - createdAt > retention;

    public static string StampFileName(string prefix)
        => $"{prefix}_{Clock.GetUtcNow().UtcDateTime:yyyyMMddHHmmss}.log";

    // Test-only entry point that returns a disposable scope.
    public static IDisposable OverrideClock(TimeProvider provider)
    {
        var previous = _clockOverride.Value;
        _clockOverride.Value = provider;
        return new ClockScope(previous);
    }

    private sealed class ClockScope(TimeProvider? previous) : IDisposable
    {
        public void Dispose() => _clockOverride.Value = previous;
    }
}
```

### Why each piece matters

- **`AsyncLocal<TimeProvider?>`** keeps the override flow-local across `async`/`await`, so tests running in parallel cannot see each other's clock. `[ThreadStatic]` would **not** flow across await points and would break that guarantee — do not use it.
- The backing field is **`readonly`** — the `AsyncLocal` container is created once; only its `.Value` changes per flow.
- **`IDisposable`** scope: the test disposes it (`using`), which restores the previous value, so the real `TimeProvider.System` is back the instant the test ends — even if it fails mid-way.
- I kept `GetUtcNow().UtcDateTime` so the `DateTimeKind.Utc` your expiry comparisons and the file-name stamp depend on is unchanged — no silent behaviour drift.

### Test usage

```csharp
using (RetentionPolicy.OverrideClock(new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2024, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero))))
{
    Assert.True(RetentionPolicy.IsExpired(new DateTimeOffset(2023, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero), TimeSpan.FromDays(30)));
}
```

### Trade-offs

Ambient context is a deliberate compromise: it adds a per-call `AsyncLocal.Value` read on the production hot path (cheap, but not free), and it is less explicit than injecting a clock — a reader must know the seam exists. Given your hard constraints (shipped static API, no container, no signature changes), it is the right tool. If you were ever free to change signatures, constructor-injected `TimeProvider` would be the cleaner long-term design — but you ruled that out, so I did not go there.
